Building a secure live student campus hub involves far more than just locks and keys. It requires a comprehensive, multi-layered approach that combines university policies, physical security measures, and advanced cybersecurity frameworks.
Based on the University of Wolverhampton's own documents and broader industry standards, here are the key security frameworks to consider for our project.
 -University-Level and Operational Frameworks
The foundation of any security initiative at Wolverhampton is its established policies. These documents outline the university's commitment and formal procedures.
•	Security Services Operational Policy: This is the core document governing security across all campuses. It outlines the 24/7 role of Security Services, their commitment to a "safe, secure, and friendly environment," and their investigation processes logged on the Incident Recording and Management Systems (IRAMS). It also clarifies the legal context for security actions, referencing the Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984 for powers like Citizen's Arrest.
•	Policy and Frameworks on Sexual Harassment and Misconduct: This framework is crucial for ensuring the hub is a safe and respectful environment. It details the university's zero-tolerance approach and its commitment to dignity, and should be read alongside the Safeguarding Policy and Dignity at Work and Study Policy. It is also balanced with the Freedom of Speech Code of Practice.
Physical and Environmental Security Frameworks
The university already employs a robust "layered" approach to physical security, which would be integral to the hub's design.
•	Access Control: This is a primary layer of defence. It includes key or swipe card access for building entrances, unique locks for individual rooms, and controlled access to communal areas. The campus-wide barrier system further regulates vehicle access.
•	24/7 Monitoring and Patrols: A dedicated security team is on duty around the clock, conducting regular patrols. This is complemented by a comprehensive CCTV system that is monitored 24/7 across all sites for the detection and prevention of crime. Intercoms at barriers and refuge points provide an additional layer of communication and security.
•	The SafeZone App: This is a critical tool for personal safety. It allows anyone on campus to quickly share their location and details with security in an emergency, effectively creating a mobile link between the individual and the university's security framework.
 Cybersecurity and Network Frameworks
For a "live" digital hub, protecting data and network integrity is paramount. Modern frameworks provide a blueprint for this.
•	Zero Trust Architecture: This is a leading cybersecurity model that operates on the principle of "never trust, always verify." Instead of assuming users inside the network are safe, Zero Trust requires continuous verification of identity, device health, and access context. Its core principles include identity-based access, least-privilege access (giving users only the access they need), and network segmentation to limit the damage from a potential breach.
•	Network Segmentation: This is a key technical control mentioned in multiple frameworks. It involves using technologies like VLANs to isolate different parts of the network. For a student hub, this could mean separating student devices from critical administrative systems and also sensitive research data to prevent an infection from spreading laterally across the network.
- Emerging and Innovative Security Concepts
Beyond traditional frameworks, there are innovative concepts that could be explored to create a smarter, safer hub.
•	"Full-Scope Security": This concept, highlighted in a recent industry standard by Huawei and the IEEE, aims to integrate digital and physical security. It includes using network infrastructure itself for security purposes. For example, Wi-Fi access points equipped with AI could be used for spatial sensing (detecting if a room is occupied without using cameras) or even for detecting hidden cameras to ensure privacy security.
